Section: int-mod

 int-mod answers the NON-NEGATIVE residue, which is not what sdiv leaves
 behind. x86-64's emit-int-mod-reg-locals takes idiv's remainder and, when it
 is negative, adds the divisor's magnitude; this helper stopped at the
 remainder, so int-mod (-7) 3 was -1 here and 2 there. Measured against
 codex/test/modprobe: three of eight sign combinations disagreed with x86, and
 it is the whole of interval-exhaustive's 62086 failures, all of them in mod,
 because iv-mod's transfer function is written for a non-negative result.
 Branchless because the offsets are then not mine to get wrong: |y| by
 negate-and-select, the corrected value computed either way, and one more
 select on the sign of the remainder.

  a64-rt-int-mod : List Integer
  a64-rt-int-mod =
   let i = []
   in let i = a64-rt-emit i (arm64-sdiv 9 0 1)
   in let i = a64-rt-emit i (arm64-msub 0 9 1 0)
   in let i = a64-rt-emit i (arm64-neg 9 1)
   in let i = a64-rt-emit i (arm64-cmp-imm 1 0)
   in let i = a64-rt-emit i (arm64-csel 9 1 9 arm64-cond-ge)
   in let i = a64-rt-emit i (arm64-add 9 0 9)
   in let i = a64-rt-emit i (arm64-cmp-imm 0 0)
   in let i = a64-rt-emit i (arm64-csel 0 0 9 arm64-cond-ge)
   in a64-rt-emit i arm64-ret

